The orange dot marks **0** — not "nothing happened," but the starting point everything else is measured from. Moving right, each dot is one more than the last. That single idea — a fixed order, one step at a time — is the seed that all of arithmetic grows from.

## 3. Not all numbers are the same kind

As soon as people needed more than "how many whole things," they needed new *kinds* of numbers: numbers for nothing, numbers for less than nothing, numbers for parts of a whole. Each new kind was invented to answer a question the old kind couldn't.

@diagram tree id="number-families" layout="tree" aria="Families of numbers, from natural numbers to real numbers"
  Numbers -> "Natural Numbers"
  Numbers -> "Whole Numbers"
  Numbers -> Integers
  Numbers -> "Rational Numbers"
  Numbers -> "Irrational Numbers"
@/diagram

| Family | Answers the question | Examples |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Natural numbers | "How many whole things?" | 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, … |
| Whole numbers | "How many, including none at all?" | 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, … |
| Integers | "What if we go below zero?" | …, −2, −1, 0, 1, 2, … |
| Rational numbers | "What about parts of a whole?" | $\frac{1}{2}$, $\frac{3}{4}$, 0.75 |
| Irrational numbers | "What about numbers that never end or repeat?" | $\pi$, $\sqrt{2}$ |

[important]
Each family sits *inside* the next one. Every natural number is also a whole number. Every whole number is also an integer. Every integer is also a rational number. Nothing is thrown away going down the table — each new family just adds numbers the smaller one couldn't reach.
[/important]

We'll open the first drawer — **natural numbers** — properly in the next lesson.

## 4. Reading and writing numbers: place value

A single symbol can only mean so much on its own. What makes our number system powerful is **place value** — the same digit means something different depending on *where* it sits.

@math
352 = 3 \times 100 + 5 \times 10 + 2 \times 1
@/math

The digit 3 isn't "three" here — sitting in the hundreds place, it's worth three hundred. Move it one place to the right and it's worth thirty. This is why $352$ and $325$ aren't the same number at all, even though they use the exact same three digits.

[definition]
**Place value** is the value a digit has because of its position in a number, not just because of what digit it is.
[/definition]

## Summary

- A number represents a quantity — it's an answer to "how many?"
- The number line puts numbers in order, evenly spaced, so we can compare and measure with them.
- Numbers come in families — natural, whole, integer, rational, irrational — each invented to answer a question the last one couldn't.
- Place value means a digit's position changes its worth: the same digit can mean 3, 30, or 300.

Next lesson: **Natural Numbers** — where counting really begins.
